4. Parasitology 163




Procedure for obtaining a stained specimen
Examine the deposit using the ¥10 objective. A smear is made on a slide by crush-
ing the skin specimen. It is fixed using methanol and stained with Giemsa stain (see
page 170).

Stained microfilariae of Onchocerca volvulus show the following features (Fig. 4.118):

— they have no sheath;

— the front end is broad;

— the body shows rigid curves;

— the tail tapers gradually and ends in a sharp curve;

— the nuclei are large and oval and stain blue-black; they are well separated and
do not extend to the tip of the tail.


Other microfilariae found in skin biopsies
Mansonella streptocerca causes an itchy dermatitis of the infected area. Its microfilariae
are found in the skin and differ from Onchocerca volvulus in the following ways
(Fig. 4.119):

— they are slightly shorter (180–240mm);

— they are less broad (5–6mm — half the width of an erythrocyte);

— the front end is not broad;

— the tail ends in a rounded crook;

— the nuclei are smaller and reach the tip of the tail.


Recommended procedures for the detection and identification of
microfilariae in blood

